15 0 obj
<< /A 48 0 R /Alt (PRISMA-style flowchart illustrating a systematic review process: initially 11,968 records identified, 2,673 retained after duplicates, 2,673 records screened, 1,662 excluded, 1,011 full-text articles assessed, 911 excluded for specified reasons, and 100 studies included in the qualitative synthesis.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 33 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 49 0 R /Alt (Flowchart labeled \215Unified Model of SID\216 showing acquired conditions producing secondary immunodeficiency, including hematological malignancies, transplantation, autoimmune therapies, HIV, immunosenescence, and others, leading to humoral failure, cell-mediated dysfunction, and innate dysregulation, which inform a clinical risk stratification framework outlining key risk factors and guiding management.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 50 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrating a proposed clinical decision pathway for SID risk stratification, detailing steps to assess immunoglobulin levels, infection history, immunosuppressive therapy, and host factors, followed by calculation of a total risk score with criteria for low, moderate, and high-risk, and associated recommendations for monitoring and intervention, with point assignments for specific conditions.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 51 0 R /Alt (Infographic compares high-income countries and low- and middle-income countries in SID care. High-income countries have advanced immunophenotyping, broad antibody testing, IVIG and SCIG access, and multidisciplinary specialists, resulting in comprehensive management. Low- and middle-income countries provide only basic lab testing, limited IgG testing, restricted immunoglobulin replacement availability, and scarce specialist resources, offering only basic and empirical care. Equity gap highlighted at the bottom.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
